This paper examines a public online exchange in which a software tester raised a methodological concern about ChatGPT transcript preservation in a sociology-oriented forum. The claim was simple: researchers studying chatbot use need full interaction records, because screenshots, memory, fragments, and selected excerpts weaken the evidential record. The initial reception largely displaced the methodological object into questions about the speaker's legitimacy, AI use, self-linking, and fit with the forum. This case is useful because it shows anti-AI boundary work operating inside a space that should, in principle, be able to recognise othering as a social process. The thread did eventually produce useful methodological language, especially around workflow, transparency, and the study of chatbot use as practice. However, the early interaction shows how quickly a research-methods issue can be reclassified as contamination by association with generative AI. The paper argues that full-chat transcript access is not a pro-AI convenience feature but an evidence-custody problem, and that AI-use stigma can itself become an obstacle to studying AI use.
